  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current approach relies on random local perturbations which may not be sufficient to escape local minima. The central idea is to use a two-stage optimization: first, an integer-structured region optimization based on pattern-driven shift dynamics, then a continuous refinement stage. This strategy directly:\n\n1. Treats step function overlap as a regional pattern optimization\n2. Parametrizes solutions by periodic intensity functions\n3. Uses exponential random shifts to explore meaningful perturbations\n4. Balances local minima exploitation with global pattern search\n\nOur implementation creates solutions by arranging {0,0.5} or {0,1} samples in a way that naturally satisfies the sum constraint. We first generate candidate solutions with specific integer patterns and then refine them. This differs from the previous continuous local search by focusing on combinatorial dynamics and exponential correlation shifts.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Objective: Maximization (for minimization) of max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Primary constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Region optimization: Candidates are {0,0.5...,1.0} arrays with block patterns\n- Stage-wise: \n  1. Identify/score periodic intensity vectors)\n  2. Local perturbations through exponential shrinkage\n  3. Projection ensures feasibility\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parent validation:\n   - If provided initial_h_values exists, use n=80 block\n   - Else generate periodic half-pattern solution\n2. Pattern generation:\n   - Use bit-level switching to produce periodic vectors\n   - For 80 points, generate 5 block patterns\n3. Score candidates with beating_transients\n4. Refinement via exponential rate transformations (`exp_rate`)\n5. Project using: scale = (n/2) / sum(h)\n6. Continuously update best solution\n7. Handle time delivery with iteration limit\n\nSearch choices:\n- 3 exponential rate possibilities: 0.3, 0.5, 0.8\n- Bit-flip strategies: single (True), adjacent (False)\n- Pattern: periodic with block sizes 6/7, 6/11, 6/13\n- Merge schedules: small amounts (0.1), intermediate (0.3), large (0.5)\n\nNumerical robustness:\n- Feasibility projection: clip values then scale\n- Error handling: early exit if projection fails\n- Graceful fallback to initial value\n\nRuntimate safeguards:\n- Track best solution continuously\n- Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) format\n- Use monotonic decay in exp_rate schedules",
